{"from": "artifact:great-isaiah-scroll", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-01-02-mixed-quotation", "grade": "B", "note": "The Great Isaiah Scroll (ca. 125 BCE) carries Isaiah 40:3, the second half of Mark's combined quotation - the quoted Isaiah text existed in Hebrew long before Mark. Graded B under the Isaiah rule for quotation claims; the scroll says nothing about the mixed attribution to Isaiah's name. Regraded B to C by architect review: a grade covers the whole claim, and the scroll carries only the Isaiah half of the combined quotation, not Malachi 3:1. Aligned C to B in consolidation B: the parallel quotation claims (jn-01-23, mt-03-03, lk-03-04-06) carry B from the same scroll, and the calibration's Isaiah-scroll example grades the exact quoted passage B; Mark's mixed attribution is the narrative's own, not what the grade asserts."}
{"from": "artifact:cave-of-letters-sandals", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-01-07-sandal-thong", "grade": "C", "note": "A pair of woman's sandals was found in the Cave of Letters (1961 dig, c. 135 CE) - leather sandals were ordinary Judean footwear. The find is Bar Kokhba era, two generations after John, and attests the object, not the untying custom or John's saying."}
{"from": "observation:judean-desert-wild-animals", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-01-13-forty-days-tempted", "grade": "C", "note": "Rock hyraxes and Nubian ibex live on the Judaean desert plateau, and Arabian leopards were there until quite recently - the wilderness region supports wild animals. This attests the setting only; it says nothing about the temptation event."}
{"from": "site:capernaum-synagogue", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-01-21-taught-capernaum-synagogue", "grade": "D", "note": "A synagogue stood at Capernaum: the standing limestone building is 4th-5th century, and Loffreda suggests the basalt foundation beneath is the 1st-century synagogue, but others read that floor as a paved market area - the first-century date is disputed, so the grade is D."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-8-eleazar", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-01-23-synagogue-exorcism", "grade": "C", "note": "Josephus saw Eleazar, 'a certain man of my own country', releasing people that were demoniacal before Vespasian, his sons and his soldiers (Ant. 8.45-48) - Jewish demon-expulsion was a living practice in the 1st century. It attests the practice, not this exorcism."}
{"from": "text:isaiah-40-3", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:bible-api-isaiah-40-3"}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-8-eleazar", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:wikisource-ant-8-eleazar"}
{"from": "observation:judean-desert-wild-animals", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:wikipedia-judean-desert"}
{"from": "artifact:cave-of-letters-sandals", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:wikipedia-cave-of-letters"}
{"from": "claim:mk-01-02-mixed-quotation", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:wikipedia-great-isaiah-scroll"}
{"from": "claim:mk-01-21-taught-capernaum-synagogue", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:wikipedia-capernaum-mark-only"}
{"from": "claim:mk-01-23-synagogue-exorcism", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:wikipedia-capernaum-mark-only"}
{"from": "claim:mk-01-02-mixed-quotation", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-03-03-isaiah-40-3-applied", "note": "Attribution difference: Matthew 3:3 quotes Isaiah 40:3 alone and names Isaiah; Mark 1:2-3 combines Malachi 3:1 with Isaiah 40:3 but still says 'as it is written in Isaiah the prophet'."}
{"from": "text:malachi-3-1", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mk-01-02-mixed-quotation", "note": "Malachi 3:1 ('Behold, I send my messenger before your face, who will prepare your way') is the first half of Mark's combined quotation, though Mark attributes both halves to Isaiah."}
{"from": "claim:mk-01-13-forty-days-tempted", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-04-02-forty-days-fasting", "note": "Mark has forty days tempted by Satan, with the wild animals and serving angels, and no fasting; Matthew 4:2 has forty days and nights of fasting and three dialogued temptations."}
{"from": "claim:mk-01-21-taught-capernaum-synagogue", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-07-28-29-scribes", "note": "The same amazement formula - teaching 'with authority, and not as the scribes' - ends the Sermon on the Mount in Matthew 7:28-29; Mark places it at the Capernaum synagogue, an occasion Matthew does not narrate (Wikipedia, Capernaum: the story 'is not contained in the Gospel of Matthew')."}
